      "id": 1,
      "text": "Shelbyville was renamed in 1837 to honor Revolutionary War hero and Kentucky governor Isaac Shelby.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shelbyville%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Settlers from Nashville, Tennessee, founded Shelbyville in the 1820s, bringing a piece of their home state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shelbyville%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original county seat of Shelby County was located in Shelbyville before moving to Center in 1866.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://shelbycountychamber.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1885 the Houston, East and West Texas Railroad reached Shelby County, connecting the area to Houston by rail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://shelbycountychamber.com/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Shelby County courthouse, built in 1866, was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1971.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
